  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Your smart home hub placement is more important than you'd think",
      "link": "https://www.engadget.com/2314367/smart-home-hub-placement-importance/",
      "description": "Keeping your smart home network functioning properly starts with understanding where to place it in relation to your smart devices and Wi-Fi router.",
      "source_name": "Engadget",
      "published": "2026-11-16T16:45:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "Smart Home Agents Make Capable but Unpredictable Grid Operators",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18395",
      "description": "arXiv:2611.18395v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: AI-enabled smart homes are becoming more common across the grid edge. However, there has not been much work on failure response. This area is critical because smart homes operate on the grid edge where failures can cascade down. If a power outage happens within a smart home, a user has to manually troubleshoot their smart home to include tasks such as power load balance and device recovery. We conduct the first resilience analysis of smart home agents across a full power outage, providing a suite of outage simulations in a live environment that measures resilience before, during, and post power outage. We show that smart home agents can act as grid operators in power outages and identify several new failure categories highlighting the current limitations across all of our models. These findings establish the groundwork for smart home agents as grid operators to increase overall resilience on the grid edge.",
      "source_name": "arXiv AI",
      "published": "2026-11-16T05:00:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "Use-case-driven ontology modeling with the NeOn methodology for ambient assisted living",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17466",
      "description": "arXiv:2611.17466v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Research on smart homes and ambient assisted living (AAL) has grown steadily over the last decade, driven largely by the ageing of the population. A smart home has to coordinate heterogeneous smart objects in order to offer services tailored to its dwellers, and this requires a shared formal description of both the dwellers and the devices. We present the ontology developed as the semantic data layer of the Virtual Home Framework in the Italian Design For All project. Its requirements are derived from two use cases: a frail elderly woman who exercises at home on a cycle-ergometer, and a designer who configures a kitchen for users with different impairments. The requirements are collected in an Ontology Requirements Specification Document and the ontology is built with the NeOn methodology, reusing and reengineering existing ontologies where possible. The resulting modular ontology describes users through the International Classification of Functioning, Disability and Health (ICF) and through their cardiorespiratory fitness; it also describes smart objects, their programs, the measurements they produce, and the comfort dimensions of the home. SWRL rules classify fitness levels, suggest exercise loads, assess indoor comfort, and identify the smart objects suited to a given impairment.",
      "source_name": "arXiv AI",
      "published": "2026-11-16T05:00:00"
    }
  ]
}